This directory contains supplemental tools for working with pcap files produced
by libbtbb.  (Actually libbtbb doesn't produce such files by itself at this
time, but you can produce them with the Ubertooth tools.

These tools are installed with libbtbb when the build finds python3 and is not
configured with ENABLE_PYTHON=OFF; the pcapdump module also needs pip.  They
can be used right out of this directory too.

pcapdump: a Python module for pcap

Pcapdump is used by btaptap to read and write pcap files.  It needs python 3.6
or newer.


btaptap: a Bluetooth keyboard sniffer

Many Bluetooth keyboards operate by default in "boot mode" which features
simplified HID messaging that can be interpreted, for example, by a PC BIOS.
Some of those keyboards also transmit these messages over unencrypted
Bluetooth.  This tool can be used to extract keystrokes from such
transmissions.

Usage: btaptap [-r pcapfile.pcap | -e ellisysfile.csv] [-c count] [-h]

-r reads a libpcap capture, either of baseband frames with the data link layer
DLT_EN10MB and the frame 20 bytes in, or of HCI traffic with
DLT_BLUETOOTH_HCI_H4.  -e reads an Ellisys CSV export.  -c stops after that
many packets.  The keystrokes go to standard output; anything that stops the
capture being read through is reported on standard error and exits non-zero.

Note that the Ubertooth tools write their captures with a Bluetooth baseband
data link layer, which btaptap does not read: it refuses such a capture rather
than decoding it.
